    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.find_isomorphism - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.find_isomorphism.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.find_isomorphism()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a node correspondence that shows two graphs are isomorphic.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.find_isomorphism()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find a node correspondence that shows two graphs are isomorphic.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.find_isomorphism()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find a node correspondence that shows two graphs are isomorphic.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind a node correspondence that shows two graphs are isomorphic.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L21611-L21753){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.find_isomorphism(\n    other,\n    node_attr=None,\n    edge_attr=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Find isomorphism](../assets/graphs/find-isomorphism.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Two directed graphs drawn differently. Graph a is a workflow where fetch points to build and test, and build points to test; graph b has nodes s1, s2, s3 with s1 pointing to s2 and s3, and s2 pointing to s3. Matching colors pair each node of a with its partner in b, and the card returns the mapping fetch to s1 (accent), build to s2 (teal), and test to s3 (violet), under which every edge of a lands on an edge of b.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nWhere `is_isomorphic()` answers yes or no, this method returns the correspondence\nitself: a dictionary that maps every node of this graph to its partner in `other`, so\nthat two nodes are joined in this graph exactly when their partners are joined in\n`other`. It is the tool for aligning two versions of the same network whose labels\ndiffer, such as matching atoms between two drawings of a molecule or nodes between two\nexports of the same system.\n\nWhen several correspondences exist (for example in a symmetric graph), one of them is\nreturned. Use `subgraph_isomorphisms()` with `other` as the pattern to list them all.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The `Graph` to compare with. It is not modified.\n\n<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node attribute name, or list of names, whose values must be equal for\n    matched nodes.\n\n<code>[edge_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional edge attribute name, or list of names, whose values must be equal for\n    matched edges.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, int] or None]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A mapping from node IDs of this graph to node IDs of `other`, or `None` if the\n    graphs are not isomorphic.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nUses the same search as `is_isomorphic()` (color refinement followed by a VF2++ style\nbacktracking search) and stops at the first complete correspondence.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_isomorphic`) \n\n:   Decide isomorphism without building the correspondence.\n\n[](`~subgraph_isomorphisms`) \n\n:   List every correspondence, or the occurrences of a pattern.\n\n[](`~canonical_labeling`) \n\n:   Number the nodes so that isomorphic graphs become identical.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe same three-step workflow, exported twice with different names. The mapping pairs\neach step of the first with its counterpart in the second:\n\n::: {#aac23512 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\na = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"fetch\", \"build\"), (\"build\", \"test\"), (\"fetch\", \"test\")], directed=True\n)\nb = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"s2\", \"s3\"), (\"s1\", \"s3\"), (\"s1\", \"s2\")], directed=True)\n\nmapping = a.find_isomorphism(b)\n{a.get_label_from_id(k): b.get_label_from_id(v) for k, v in mapping.items()}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'fetch': 's1', 'build': 's2', 'test': 's3'}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Two numberings of caffeine\n\n`molecules()[\"caffeine\"]` numbers the atoms of each element in turn. Chemists number the\nring atoms of caffeine 1 to 9 by the scheme for purines, which is where its name,\n1,3,7-trimethylxanthine, comes from. Here is the same molecule written that way, with the\ntwo oxygens and three methyl carbons numbered after the rings:\n\n::: {#3166e436 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\ncaffeine = ec.molecules()[\"caffeine\"]\npurine = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"N1\", \"C2\"), (\"C2\", \"N3\"), (\"N3\", \"C4\"), (\"C4\", \"C5\"), (\"C5\", \"C6\"), (\"C6\", \"N1\"),\n     (\"C4\", \"N9\"), (\"N9\", \"C8\"), (\"C8\", \"N7\"), (\"N7\", \"C5\"),\n     (\"C2\", \"O10\"), (\"C6\", \"O11\"), (\"N1\", \"C12\"), (\"N3\", \"C13\"), (\"N7\", \"C14\")]\n)\npurine.set_node_attrs(\"element\", {atom: atom[0] for atom in purine.get_node_df()[\"label\"]})\n\nmapping = caffeine.find_isomorphism(purine, node_attr=\"element\")\npl.DataFrame(\n    {\n        \"caffeine\": [caffeine.get_label_from_id(k) for k in mapping],\n        \"purine\": [purine.get_label_from_id(v) for v in mapping.values()],\n    }\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (14, 2)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>caffeine</th><th>purine</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;C1&quot;</td><td>&quot;C14&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;N1&quot;</td><td>&quot;N7&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C2&quot;</td><td>&quot;C8&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;N2&quot;</td><td>&quot;N9&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C3&quot;</td><td>&quot;C4&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&hellip;</td><td>&hellip;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C6&quot;</td><td>&quot;C2&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;O2&quot;</td><td>&quot;O10&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;N4&quot;</td><td>&quot;N3&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C7&quot;</td><td>&quot;C13&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C8&quot;</td><td>&quot;C12&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe three nitrogens with methyl groups, N1, N3 and N4 here, are N7, N1 and N3 in the\npurine scheme. Caffeine has no symmetry, so this is the only mapping. Side by side, each\natom keeps its bonds under its new name: the methyl carbon C1 on N1 is C14 on N7:\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#be5f3aa0 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncaffeine.plot(\n    color_by=\"element\", label_by=\"label\", legend=False, seed=1, width=360, height=360\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"360\" height=\"360\" viewBox=\"5.6 15.6 348.9 348.9\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI